  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nIt relatively quiet today as the surface boundary remains \ndraped west-east to our south. This front remains mostly \nstationary under northwest flow aloft, though it should start \nto dip further south as an area of weak, high surface pressure \ncontinues to spread across the Upper MS Valley. Any sort of kink\nin the midlevel flow could produce an isolated storm this \nevening given the few thousand Joules of CAPE, though forecast \nsoundings highlight that a shallow moisture profile should limit\nany activity for the rest of the day. The latest update to the \nSPC Day 1 SWO removed the Marginal Risk from most of Minnesota \nand western Wisconsin.\n\nTonight into Wednesday remains mostly dry ahead of another \nchance for nocturnal storms Wednesday night into Thursday. \nLatest guidance favors storms developing across central SoDak \nand eastern Nebraska during the evening, then diving southeast \nthrough northern Iowa. This mainly develops out of a weak mid \nlevel wave that becomes enhanced along the nose of a 700hPa jet.\nThis will become a similar situation to last night where we are\ntracking the location of the quasi-stationary front and the \ndeveloping shortwave. A broad Marginal Risk remains in place \nfor Wednesday's severe weather outlook, though for our area, we \nexpect the main storm threat to be focused across southwest and \nsouthern MN. Given CAPE values will be much higher further south\ninto Iowa, any convection that does develop may even avoid much\nof southern MN altogether as it follows the gradient of higher \nCAPE values.\n\nA slightly cooler airmass is expected to move in as we head into\nthe end of the week with afternoon highs generally in the mid \n70s to low 80s Thursday onwards. A quick shot of increased \nmoisture is still expected Friday into Saturday as an area of \nlow pressure develops to the W/SW, and PWAT values approach 2\" \nacross southern MN. There is still notable spread across \nensemble guidance, though this will bring our best chance of \nsteady precip over the next 7 days. The latest NBM QPF amounts \nsuggest a number of areas could see over an inch of rainfall \nover a 30-36 hour period, especially across southern MN. \n\nHeading into next week, we likely remain under northwest flow, \nthough a building ridge across the western US may begin to \ninfluence a warming trend and occasional storm chances.",
